<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<configuration>
  <!-- nuget.org ONLY. Godot.NET.Sdk / GodotSharp 4.6.3 are published there (verified 2026-06), which is what
       CI has always restored from.

       WHY THE "godot-editor" LOCAL SOURCE IS GONE (2026-08-01, bootstrap Phase 0). This file used to add
       C:\Program Files\Godot\GodotSharp\Tools\nupkgs as a second source, to guarantee the packages matched
       the installed editor exactly and to work offline. The cost was out of all proportion to that benefit:
       NuGet treats a MISSING LOCAL SOURCE AS A FATAL ERROR, not a warning, so `dotnet restore` failed
       outright on every machine that did not have Godot installed at that exact Windows path — which is
       every fresh clone, on every platform, including Windows. The suite could not even be restored, let
       alone run.

       .github/workflows/ci.yml already worked around it with `dotnet nuget remove source godot-editor`
       before restoring, so CI has never actually used the local source. Removing it here makes the default
       path the one that was already proven, and removes that workaround's reason to exist.

       IF YOU WANT THE EXACT-EDITOR-MATCH / OFFLINE PROPERTY BACK: add it per-machine rather than committing
       it, so it cannot break anyone else's clone. Run `dotnet nuget add source` against the editor's
       nupkgs folder (C:\Program Files\Godot\GodotSharp\Tools\nupkgs) with a name of your choosing; that
       writes to the user-level NuGet.Config.

       A repo-level nuget.local.config would work too, but it must stay gitignored: the failure above came
       from a machine-specific path being committed.

       (Note for future editors of this file: an XML comment may not contain a double hyphen, which is why
       the command above is described rather than written out with its flags.) -->
  <packageSources>
    <clear />
    <add key="nuget.org" value="https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json" />
  </packageSources>
</configuration>
